# Native content-defined file chunks and lifecycle fixes

Native encrypted snapshots now use the authenticated repository's degree-53 polynomial with the pinned restic/chunker v0.4.0 boundary contract: a 64-byte rolling window, seed/reset behavior, a 512 KiB minimum, an 8 MiB maximum, and average 20-bit boundary selection. Each file starts a fresh chunker. SHA-256 identities identify the resulting plaintext chunks; already stored chunks are authenticated and reused across compression modes. Application-format `.rea-backup` snapshots retain their fixed 1 MiB format.

This is source-assisted engineering. The [source preservation manifest](results/rabin-source-study/preservation-manifest.json) retains exact original Go source, its [BSD-2-Clause notice](results/rabin-source-study/fixed-stage/references/LICENSE), dependency binding, untouched author stage, prototype, failed harness probes, independent reviews and root repairs. The original restic executable is used by tests only. The application calls its own chunker, file adapter and encrypted repository writer.

The authenticated original restic 0.19.1 executable matched 30 file cases and 126 ordered chunk length/plaintext SHA-256 entries across three controlled valid polynomials. Cases include inserted and mutated data, random data, forced maximum boundaries, zero data, minimum boundaries, tiny and empty files. The original CLI does not expose rolling cut fingerprints; those are checked only against retained upstream zero-byte vectors. Four additional original commands check and restore the staged compressed/native snapshots. These finite comparisons do not cover custom chunker average/minimum/maximum options, reset/reader APIs, all files or full restic behavior.

Independent review reproduced two inherited lifecycle defects: source cancellation after an awaited stat could reach a sink or final success, and a deadline checked after immutable publication could report failure after committing an object. An intermediate repair passed 20 lifecycle checks but still started later pre-commit I/O after expired mkdir/open/write awaits. Its failures and three diagnostics remain preserved. The final independent review passed the same 20 checks plus all three strict diagnostics against the repaired sources. Production tests now exercise the actual application modules, rather than historical stage copies.

The writer checks deadlines after awaited temporary-directory creation, open, write and sync, then immediately before the immutable link. Cleanup closes opened handles and resolves confined own temporary paths independently of an elapsed operation deadline. A committed operation retains its result and reports genuine cleanup warnings; a primary failure retains its own error and additional cleanup details. Own lock identity remains available until unlink succeeds or absence is verified. Other clients' locks and pre-existing temporary files are never deleted by this cleanup.

The file adapter reads private 512 KiB blocks, isolates every emitted chunk from consumer mutation, and checks the open file and current path for device/inode, size and nanosecond modification/change times before and after sinks. It checks cancellation and remaining deadlines before a sink and final success. The native API retains its two-minute cooperative deadline; it does not expose cancellation controls. Blocked filesystem work and sink promises cannot be forcibly preempted.

Limits remain 64 MiB per file and 512 MiB per snapshot. Calculated capture buffers total at most 17 MiB (8 MiB assembly, up to 8 MiB isolated output and two 512 KiB input/read buffers). This excludes caller-retained data, compression/encryption/pack work and runtime allocations; it is not an RSS cap. Source mutation checks are not a filesystem snapshot or proof against every path-replacement race. Aborted captures can leave immutable unindexed packs. The writer now aggregates distinct data blobs into sequential data-only packs with a 16 MiB target; see [pack aggregation and operation ownership](PACKS.md). Original concurrent/randomized batching, repository repair, remote backends, native retention, metadata families and all command-line features remain open.

No original speed/resource comparison, crash/power-loss durability guarantee, security certification, complete feature parity or universal superiority has been established. Current application verification is recorded separately from the preserved stage and independent review receipts.
